---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { fixFormUrl, FIX_EMAIL } from '../lib/group-requests.mjs';
export const page = { door: "about", label: "About this site", summary: "Who runs it, how the AI works, and how to check our work" };
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const repo = 'https://github.com/david-shortman/mon-county-community-hub';
const doc = (p) => `${repo}/blob/main/${p}`;
const tellUs = fixFormUrl({ page: Astro.url.pathname });
---
<Base title="About" description="Who runs Everything Morgantown, how the AI behind it works, and how you can check our work.">
  <h1>About this project</h1>
  <p class="lede">Everything Morgantown helps you find help, services, and dates that matter in Morgantown and Monongalia County. AI does most of the research and writing. A real person is responsible for it.</p>

  <h2>Who's behind this</h2>
  <p>I'm David McCoy. I moved to West Virginia in 2022, and I married my wild and wonderful wife in 2024, which keeps me here. I'm a software developer, and I've built software for nonprofits and businesses.</p>
  <p>This is a volunteer project. It has no funding and makes no money. There are no ads, and it doesn't ask for donations. I'm not looking for attention. My name is here only so you know a real person is behind it.</p>
  <p>Here's why I'm doing it. Some groups are using technology to leave people behind. AI tools can now work around the clock: looking things up, checking facts, and keeping a lot of work moving. Scammers and shady outfits are already using that. I think it should be working for us instead, and for the people and groups already doing good work here.</p>
  <p>So this site is a test of how far that can go for one community. I want to see people's lives get better, and see people own more of their own time. I'd love for local leaders to say they could help more people, and for more things to become simple, easy, even boring. If it works, I hope local leaders or other towns take the idea and run with it.</p>

  <h2>How you can check us</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Every listing shows where it came from.</strong> It links to its source, usually the group's own page, and shows the date we last checked it.</li>
    <li><strong>You deal with the group, not us.</strong> When you sign up, call, or give, you do it with them. This site has no sign-up forms and takes no payments. It never asks for your name. Our one form, for fixes and removals, asks for an email only if you want a reply.</li>
    <li><strong>Any group can ask us to fix or remove its listing.</strong> Use our <a href={fixFormUrl({ page: Astro.url.pathname })}>fix or removal form</a>, or email <a href={`mailto:${FIX_EMAIL}`}>{FIX_EMAIL}</a>. You don't need an account, and what you send is not posted. The "Report a problem" link on each listing opens the same form.</li>
    <li><strong>Every listing shows its oldest checked date.</strong> If any of its sources was last checked more than 6 months ago, the listing says it may be out of date.</li>
    <li><strong>The work is public.</strong> The code, the data, the rules the AI follows, and a log of what it built and why are all on <a href={repo}>GitHub</a>. Planning notes and notes about specific people are kept private.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we use AI</h2>
  <p>Most of this site is researched and written by an AI assistant (Claude, made by Anthropic). The AI only repeats facts it found in a public source, and every listing links to that source and shows the date it was last checked. A person, not the AI, oversees the project, and anyone can see our work and our mistakes on GitHub. AI can still get things wrong, so please confirm details with the group before you act, and tell us if something is off.</p>
  <p>We don't use AI-made pictures of real places or people. No page speaks for a government office or nonprofit: we describe what they offer and link to them.</p>

  <h2>How it runs</h2>
  <p>Every day, AI agents (Claude, made by Anthropic) work through the same steps. They look for places where information about life here is hard to find or out of date. They pick the most useful fix and build it. Then they check whether it helped and write down what they did and why.</p>
  <p>The AI publishes most changes on its own, with no person looking first. Every change is a public "pull request" on GitHub, with a note on what changed and why. Before a change goes live, another AI run checks it against our rules for sources, plain words, fairness and privacy. When something is wrong, it gets fixed in the open, and the fix shows up in the public log.</p>
  <p>The AI follows these rules every time:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Every fact needs a public source</strong> and the date it was checked. If the AI can't find a source, the fact stays off the site.</li>
    <li><strong>Describe, don't take sides.</strong> No candidates, parties, or favorite businesses. Voting information points to the West Virginia Secretary of State.</li>
    <li><strong>No personal information</strong> about private people, ever. We don't track you. Directions buttons open Google Maps, and nothing goes to Google unless you tap one.</li>
    <li><strong>Saved copies stay on your phone.</strong> So pages can open with no signal, your browser keeps copies of the Help now page, the pages for a hard moment (Tonight, food help, utility bills, after a storm, mental health, drugs and alcohol, protective orders), the home page, the main topic pages and Dates, plus pages you open. A saved copy shows the date it was saved, and old copies are deleted: after 3 days for pages that change daily, 30 days for the hard moment pages, and 90 days for the rest. The copies stay on your phone and are not sent anywhere. To remove them, clear this site's data in your browser settings.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Some things the AI is never allowed to do on its own. They always wait for my yes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Contacting any person or group, or speaking for the project</li>
    <li>Sending messages or alerts to the public</li>
    <li>Spending money, or signing up for paid services or outside accounts</li>
    <li>Handling passwords or other secrets</li>
    <li>Collecting personal information beyond what a feature needs</li>
    <li>Anything partisan</li>
    <li>Changing the project's own rules</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How items are chosen and checked</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chosen:</strong> the AI tries to answer real questions people here ask, like "where can I get food today?", and fixes the places where the answer is hard to find, out of date, or hard to read on a phone.</li>
    <li><strong>Checked:</strong> every phone number, hour, rule and date must be on a public page the AI opened and read that same day. It never fills a gap from memory. If the source doesn't say it, it stays off the site.</li>
    <li><strong>Kept close to the source:</strong> who a program is for, dates and costs are written as close to the source's own words as plain language allows. We never tell you that you qualify. The program decides.</li>
    <li><strong>When sources disagree:</strong> we say who says what, and point you to your doctor or the office that decides.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The full rules are in <a href={doc('content/README.md')}>our content guide</a>.</p>
  <p>My part: I guide the project at the top. Some changes always wait for me to check them before they go live:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>pages and listings about shelter, crisis help, food and benefits</li>
    <li>changes to the rules</li>
    <li>anything about money or outside accounts</li>
    <li>anything that speaks for the project</li>
  </ul>
  <p>I look over other pages when I can, after they are up.</p>
  <p class="note">AI can still get things wrong. Please check details with the group before you act, and <a href={tellUs}>tell us</a> if something is off.</p>

  <h2 id="corrections">How we fix mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>What counts:</strong> a wrong fact, a broken or wrong link, a detail that is out of date, or wording that takes a side.</li>
    <li><strong>How to tell us:</strong> the "Report a problem" link on the listing, or "Tell us" at the bottom of any page. If you work at the place or help people find services, say so on the form, and we read your report first. No names needed.</li>
    <li><strong>If it could hurt someone</strong> (a wrong crisis line, shelter, deadline or benefit rule), we take the item down until it is re-checked.</li>
    <li><strong>How we fix it:</strong> we re-read the source, fix the listing, and update its checked date.</li>
    <li><strong>How we show it:</strong> every fix to a fact, link or date is listed on our <a href={`${base}/corrections/`}>Corrections page</a>, with the date. Typos are fixed without a note. Every change is also public on GitHub.</li>
    <li><strong>What we can't act on:</strong> opinions about which groups to list, changes without a source we can check, or anything that would add private personal information.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Read more</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={`${base}/how-it-works/`}>How this site works</a>: how a listing gets here, who does what, and the checks that run on their own.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/the-idea/`}>The idea behind this site</a>: what we're trying to do, why it can work, and how to check each part.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/hard-questions/`}>Hard questions</a>: straight answers about who runs this, why AI, and where we fall short today.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/how-we-check/`}>How we check</a>: what counts as a source, how often we recheck, and how that compares with 211.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/partners/`}>For groups and helpers</a>: how to fix a listing, reuse the data, and what we will and won't do.</li>
    <li><a href={doc('docs/CHARTER.md')}>The project charter</a>: the mission, goals, and the limits on what the AI may do.</li>
    <li><a href={doc('docs/COORDINATION.md')}>How we work</a>: who does what, and when a change waits for a person.</li>
    <li><a href={doc('CLAUDE.md')}>The AI's standing instructions</a> for this project.</li>
    <li><a href={doc('docs/playbook/trust-and-ai.md')}>Trust and AI</a>: the practices we follow and where they come from.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/corrections/`}>Corrections</a>: what we got wrong and fixed.</li>
    <li><a href={`${repo}/tree/main/docs/build-log`}>Build log</a>: what was built, and why.</li>
    <li><a href={doc('docs/decisions.md')}>Decision log</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
